{"task":"transcribe","language":"en","duration":266.88,"text":"I've been working on developing artifact detection models for physiological waveforms. Up until recently, the focus had been arterial blood pressure data, and we developed, we wrote a paper that evaluated a slew of different methods for doing artifact detection on ABP. It included a bunch of published methods that came before our paper and adaptations of different methods and a couple of new methods, specifically ones that leverage pre-trained image classifiers that have been adapted to work on images of the physiological waveforms. Those pre-trained models perform the best, and so those are the ones I want to focus on for the ECG paper. The task for the ECG paper is I want more scope down than what we did for the ABP paper. So right now what I'm kind of thinking is focus in on the image methods since they do so well. Do the ensemble, sensitivity analysis, and potentially patient-specific fine-tuning, but then compare it against some standard that we can adapt for our purposes. And to that goal, I'm interested in using something, a paper that was published a while ago in 2022 titled Robustness of Electrocardiogram Signal Quality Indices. I want to use those signal quality indices to train machine learning models and use that as a baseline for artifact detection. I've attached the original draft of the ABP paper and I'm going to be attaching some of the scripts and code that I've written for training the ECG models. To give you a little bit more background on the data set, we have 65 patients from which we've sampled 10,000 pulses. The annotation approach is similar to what's described for ABP. The main difference is we didn't pursue individual beat level segmentation here, mainly because we ran into some challenges with actually doing that reliably over large recordings. So instead we decided to chunk up the recording into 10 second segments and classify those as artifact. Of that 10,000 segment data set, each pulse is 10 seconds long. We're using 7,000 of them for training. To be specific, 7,490 for training. For testing, we're using 1700. And for validation, we're using 1700. And these splits were done similarly in the ABP paper. So we're ensuring that all of the we're trying to make sure there is a good balance of artifact versus not artifact in all the data sets. For annotation, those were handled by human annotators. We did the same four-point scale in this case, but there was no segmentation errors. And then we binarized those into artifact versus normal. The goal of reaching really high sensitivity is still true in this paper, so that holds for both of them. The key difference here is that instead of training the image models on plots, I'm training them on spectrograms of the ECG waveforms. So all of that said, I want your advice in performing my hyperparameter tuning runs. I want you to review the code I've given and the background from the previous papers and help me come up with a good test matrix for the different ranges of hyperparameters I'm going to tune for ResNet and EfficientNet, including both the shallow and full fine-tuned versions.","segments":[{"id":0,"start":0.0,"end":5.12,"text":"I've been working on developing artifact detection models for physiological waveforms."},{"id":1,"start":5.44,"end":19.04,"text":"Up until recently, the focus had been arterial blood pressure data, and we developed, we wrote a paper that evaluated a slew of different methods for doing artifact detection on ABP."},{"id":2,"start":19.36,"end":36.64,"text":"It included a bunch of published methods that came before our paper and adaptations of different methods and a couple of new methods, specifically ones that leverage pre-trained image classifiers that have been adapted to work on images of the physiological waveforms."},{"id":3,"start":36.96,"end":46.08,"text":"Those pre-trained models perform the best, and so those are the ones I want to focus on for the ECG paper."},{"id":4,"start":46.4,"end":53.92,"text":"The task for the ECG paper is I want more scope down than what we did for the ABP paper."},{"id":5,"start":54.24,"end":61.76,"text":"So right now what I'm kind of thinking is focus in on the image methods since they do so well."},{"id":6,"start":62.4,"end":74.08,"text":"Do the ensemble, sensitivity analysis, and potentially patient-specific fine-tuning, but then compare it against some standard that we can adapt for our purposes."},{"id":7,"start":74.4,"end":93.28,"text":"And to that goal, I'm interested in using something, a paper that was published a while ago in 2022 titled Robustness of Electrocardiogram Signal Quality Indices."},{"id":8,"start":93.60000000000001,"end":101.76,"text":"I want to use those signal quality indices to train machine learning models and use that as a baseline for artifact detection."},{"id":9,"start":104.0,"end":116.32000000000001,"text":"I've attached the original draft of the ABP paper and I'm going to be attaching some of the scripts and code that I've written for training the ECG models."},{"id":10,"start":116.72,"end":124.56,"text":"To give you a little bit more background on the data set, we have 65 patients from which we've sampled 10,000 pulses."},{"id":11,"start":124.88000000000001,"end":127.92,"text":"The annotation approach is similar to what's described for ABP."},{"id":12,"start":128.08,"end":138.48,"text":"The main difference is we didn't pursue individual beat level segmentation here, mainly because we ran into some challenges with actually doing that reliably over large recordings."},{"id":13,"start":138.72,"end":145.20000000000002,"text":"So instead we decided to chunk up the recording into 10 second segments and classify those as artifact."},{"id":14,"start":146.16,"end":150.88,"text":"Of that 10,000 segment data set, each pulse is 10 seconds long."},{"id":15,"start":151.76,"end":155.04,"text":"We're using 7,000 of them for training."},{"id":16,"start":160.88,"end":164.4,"text":"To be specific, 7,490 for training."},{"id":17,"start":164.72,"end":167.6,"text":"For testing, we're using 1700."},{"id":18,"start":167.76,"end":170.16,"text":"And for validation, we're using 1700."},{"id":19,"start":172.72,"end":176.32,"text":"And these splits were done similarly in the ABP paper."},{"id":20,"start":176.48,"end":187.20000000000002,"text":"So we're ensuring that all of the we're trying to make sure there is a good balance of artifact versus not artifact in all the data sets."},{"id":21,"start":187.44,"end":190.48,"text":"For annotation, those were handled by human annotators."},{"id":22,"start":190.8,"end":196.0,"text":"We did the same four-point scale in this case, but there was no segmentation errors."},{"id":23,"start":196.64000000000001,"end":201.92000000000002,"text":"And then we binarized those into artifact versus normal."},{"id":24,"start":203.52,"end":210.88,"text":"The goal of reaching really high sensitivity is still true in this paper, so that holds for both of them."},{"id":25,"start":212.16,"end":220.24,"text":"The key difference here is that instead of training the image models on plots, I'm training them on spectrograms of the ECG waveforms."},{"id":26,"start":220.88,"end":231.12,"text":"So all of that said, I want your advice in performing my hyperparameter tuning runs."},{"id":27,"start":233.28,"end":252.08,"text":"I want you to review the code I've given and the background from the previous papers and help me come up with a good test matrix for the different ranges of hyperparameters I'm going to tune for ResNet and EfficientNet, including both the shallow and full fine-tuned versions."}]}